A CALL'S FORMS COME FROM THE HOST, not from a list anybody typed here. Every line below stands
verified against TiddlyWiki's own parser: the `macrocall` code inside transclude.js resolves a `<<x>>` invocation
the same way a `\procedure`, `\function` or `\widget` binds — the CALL side of the vocabulary owns
every shape here, whatever kind of thing answers to the name.

! Inline and block calls

An inline call stands beside prose: this text calls <<greeting>> mid-sentence.

A block call stands alone on its own line:

<<greeting>>

! Positional parameters

<<greeting one two three>>

<<greeting "a double quoted value">>

<<greeting 'a single quoted value'>>

<<greeting """a triple quoted value""">>

<<greeting [[a bracketed value]]>>

! Named parameters

<<greeting who=world>>

<<greeting who="a double quoted value">>

<<greeting who='a single quoted value'>>

<<greeting who="""a triple quoted value""">>

<<greeting who=[[a bracketed value]]>>

! A widget attribute's value can itself be a call

<$button tooltip=<<greeting>>>go</$button>

! A call nested inside another call's parameter

A NAMED parameter admits a nested call as its value: <<outer inner=<<greeting>>>>.

! The `$`-prefixed widget form calls the same way `<<x>>` does

`<$transclude>`'s `$variable` attribute names the call, and every OTHER attribute becomes an
ordinary parameter — the same params a `<<x>>` call passes positionally or by name.

<$transclude $variable="greeting"/>

<$transclude $variable="greeting" who="world"/>

! A function is a call too, and reads the same way

<<isEven n:4>> and <$transclude $variable="isEven" n="4"/> stand in the same family: a named
thing, invoked by `<<x>>` or by `$variable`, taking parameters either way.
